<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mataʻafa Iosefo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1832–1912</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mataafa Iosefo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mataʻafa Iosefo was a Samoan high chief and claimant to the kingship of Samoa in the late 19th century. His rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malietoa-laupepa">Malietoa Laupepa</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malietoa-tanumafili-i">Malietoa Tanumafili I</a> led to wars in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> intervened. After the partition of 1899 the Germans recognised him as paramount chief of German Samoa until his death in 1912.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mataafa-iosefo.jpg" alt="Mataʻafa Iosefo" width="320" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Andrew · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samoan_Paramount_chief_Mataafa_Iosefa,_1896.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
